#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
tf_wp_cleanup.py - find, clean and RECOVER hacked WordPress sites on shared hosting (cPanel).

Works on the folder you are standing in (and below). Run it from the site's root
folder (the one with wp-config.php, wp-admin, wp-includes). Keep the script in your
HOME folder (not public_html) and call it as ~/tf_wp_cleanup.py.

ONE-COMMAND RECOVERY (take a cPanel backup first):
    cd ~/public_html/site1
    tf_wp_cleanup --recover
  = restore missing/modified core + plugin files from official ZIPs
  + quarantine PHP in uploads / unknown PHP in core folders
  + strip malicious auto_prepend_file lines
  + rewrite .htaccess with the standard WordPress block (--htaccess)
  + fix file permissions (777/666 cause 500 errors)
  + explain what is still broken (reads your error_log)

STEP BY STEP
    tf_wp_cleanup                 scan only, changes nothing (dry run)
    tf_wp_cleanup --fix           apply the safe fixes from the scan
    tf_wp_cleanup --fix --aggressive   also quarantine unknown plugin PHP + signature hits
    tf_wp_cleanup --diagnose      read error logs, explain the 500 error
Recovery tools (each acts immediately and is reversible):
    --htaccess                  rewrite .htaccess with the standard WordPress block (old one backed up)
                                (also works as --reset-htaccess; --rewrite-base /sub/ for subfolder sites, --keep-php-handler keeps cPanel PHP version lines)
    --rebuild-config            rebuild a clean wp-config.php from the old settings (--db-name/--db-user/
                                --db-password/--db-host/--table-prefix if the old one is gone)
    --debug on|off              log PHP errors to wp-content/debug.log, then read them with --diagnose
    --check-admin               read-only: check admin roles, list users (flags accounts made recently)
    --fix-admin USER            give USER (login or ID) the administrator role; rebuilds broken roles
    --fix-perms                 dirs 755, files 644, wp-config.php 600
    --disable-all-plugins       rename wp-content/plugins -> plugins.disabled   (--enable-all-plugins undoes)
    --disable-plugin SLUG       rename one plugin folder to SLUG.disabled (rename back to undo)
    --disable-theme SLUG        same for a theme
    --quarantine-file FILE      move one file to quarantine (repeatable)
    --strip-conf FILE           remove auto_prepend/append_file lines from one .user.ini/php.ini/.htaccess
Undo anything the script moved/replaced (nothing is ever deleted):
    --exclude PATH              (any mode) ignore a folder such as an old backup: --exclude bkp
    --list-quarantine
    --purge-quarantine DAYS            permanently delete quarantine runs older than DAYS (asks first; --yes for cron)
    --undo-file /full/path/to/file     put one file back as it was
    --undo-all [RUN]                   put back everything from a run (this also brings malware back!)
"""
